Supreme People's Court

Notice of the Supreme People's Court on Issuing the Interim Provisions on Judicial Expertise Work of the People's Courts

Fa Fa [2001] No. 23

The High People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government, the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army, and the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the High People's Court of Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region:

The Interim Provisions on Judicial Expertise Work of the People's Courts are hereby issued to you for your earnest implementation in conjunction with the actual work, so as to standardize the judicial expertise work of the people's courts. Please report any problems encountered during implementation to this Court in a timely manner.

November 16, 2001

Interim Provisions on Judicial Expertise Work of the People's Courts

Chapter I General Provisions

Article 1 These Provisions are formulated in accordance with the Criminal Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Organic Law of the People's Courts of the People's Republic of China, and other laws, for the purpose of standardizing the judicial expertise work of the people's courts.

Article 2 For the purposes of these Provisions, "judicial expertise" refers to the activity of inspection, identification, and assessment of specialized issues, carried out by a person with specialized knowledge appointed or entrusted by a people's court, either ex officio or upon application by a party or other participants in the proceedings, in order to ascertain the facts of a case during the litigation process.

Article 3 Judicial expertise shall adhere to the following principles:
(1) Lawfulness, independence, and openness;
(2) Objectivity, scientificity, and accuracy;
(3) Civility, fairness, and efficiency.

Article 4 For any case requiring judicial expertise, the expertise shall be conducted by the judicial expertise institution of the people's court, or be uniformly entrusted to an external institution by the judicial expertise institution of the people's court.

Article 5 The Supreme People's Court shall guide the judicial expertise work of local people's courts at all levels, and a higher people's court shall guide the judicial expertise work of lower people's courts.

Chapter II Judicial Expertise Institutions and Experts

Article 6 The Supreme People's Court, each High People's Court, and qualified Intermediate People's Courts shall establish independent judicial expertise institutions. The establishment of a new judicial expertise institution shall be subject to the approval of the Supreme People's Court.

The judicial expertise institution of the Supreme People's Court shall be the Judicial Expertise Center of the People's Courts, which may establish branches as needed for its work.

Article 7 Rights of experts:
(1) To understand the case, and to request the client to provide materials necessary for the expertise;
(2) To inspect the scene, conduct relevant examinations, and inquire with the parties concerned in the expertise. Where necessary, to apply to the people's court to collect expertise materials ex officio, to decide on the expertise method and handle the examination materials;
(3) To independently expound his or her expertise opinion; where he or she disagrees with the opinion of another expert, he or she may choose not to sign the expertise report;
(4) To refuse to accept an assignment that violates legal provisions.

Article 8 Obligations of experts:
(1) To respect science and adhere to professional ethics;
(2) To maintain the confidentiality of the case;
(3) To issue the expert conclusion in a timely manner;
(4) To appear in court according to law to read out the expert conclusion and answer questions related to the expertise.

Article 9 An expert shall withdraw in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The expert is a party to the case, or a close relative of a party;
(2) A close relative of the expert has an interest in the case;
(3) The expert has served as a witness, defense counsel, or litigation representative in the case;
(4) Any other circumstance that may affect the accuracy of the expertise.

Chapter III Assignment and Acceptance

Article 10 The judicial expertise institution of a people's court at any level shall accept the assignment of judicial expertise from its own court and from lower people's courts. A lower people's court may assign judicial expertise level by level to the judicial expertise institution of a higher people's court.

Article 11 Judicial expertise shall be assigned in written form, specifying the purpose and requirements of the expertise, and providing the necessary case explanation materials and expertise materials.

Article 12 The judicial expertise institution shall decide whether to accept the assignment within three days. If the assignment is not accepted, the reason shall be explained to the client.

Article 13 After accepting an assignment, the judicial expertise institution may, depending on the circumstances, conduct the expertise itself, or organize experts, collaborate with scientific research institutions, or entrust an expert randomly selected from the relevant expert register to conduct the expertise.

Article 14 Where a new expertise is required due to any of the following circumstances, the people's court shall assign the new expertise to the judicial expertise institution of the higher court:
(1) The expert does not possess the relevant expertise qualification;
(2) The expertise procedure does not comply with legal provisions;
(3) The expert conclusion contradicts other evidence;
(4) The expertise materials are false, or the original expertise method is flawed;
(5) The expert should have withdrawn but did not, and there are differing opinions on his or her expert conclusion;
(6) There are multiple different expert conclusions in the same case;
(7) There is evidence proving the existence of factors affecting the accuracy of the expert's expertise.

Article 15 A judicial expertise institution may, upon the assignment of a people's court, conduct documentary review of materials such as expertise reports, inspection reports, records of inquest and examination, medical records, and accounting materials that are intended to be used as evidence.

Chapter IV Inspection and Expertise

Article 16 Expertise work shall generally be carried out in the following steps:
(1) Review the expertise assignment letter;
(2) Examine the submitted materials and objects, and review relevant technical materials;
(3) Formulate an expertise plan in accordance with technical specifications;
(4) Make detailed records of the expertise activities;
(5) Issue the expertise report.

Article 17 For expertise that involves consumption of examination materials, the client shall be notified. Where necessary, the client shall issue an authorization letter for handling the examination materials.

Article 18 When collecting samples for inspection and for expertise, the client, party, or agent shall be notified to be present.

Article 19 When conducting a physical examination, if the examinee and the expert are of opposite sexes, an additional female staff member shall be present.

Article 20 For expertise that is difficult or involves multiple disciplines, the opinions of relevant experts may be sought before issuing the expert conclusion.

Chapter V Time Limit for Expertise, Suspension of Expertise, and Termination of Expertise

Article 21 The time limit for expertise refers to the period from the date of deciding to accept an assigned expertise to the date of issuing the expertise report.

General judicial expertise shall be completed within thirty working days; difficult judicial expertise shall be completed within sixty working days.

Article 22 In any of the following circumstances affecting the time limit for expertise, the expertise shall be suspended:
(1) The examinee or other examined object is in an unstable state, affecting the expert conclusion;
(2) The examinee cannot undergo examination at the designated time and place;
(3) Special examinations require an appointment or waiting for examination results;
(4) Additional expertise materials are required.

Article 23 In any of the following circumstances, the expertise may be terminated:
(1) The necessary expertise materials cannot be obtained;
(2) The person being examined or the examinee refuses to cooperate with the examination, and still does not cooperate after efforts;
(3) The lawsuit is withdrawn or the case is concluded by mediation during the expertise process;
(4) Other circumstances make the expertise impossible to proceed.

If an expert cannot complete the expertise within the specified time limit due to suspension, termination, or other special circumstances, he or she shall apply to the judicial expertise institution for an extension or termination. The judicial expertise institution shall decide whether to suspend or terminate, and if it decides to suspend or terminate, shall notify the client in writing.

Chapter VI Miscellaneous

Article 24 Where staff members of the judicial expertise institution of a people's court cause errors in expertise or wrong cases due to engaging in malpractices for personal gain or gross negligence, their liability shall be pursued by reference to the Measures for Investigating the Liability of Adjudicative Personnel of the People's Courts for Illegal Adjudication (Trial) and the Measures for Imposing Disciplinary Sanctions on Adjudicative Personnel of the People's Courts (Trial). Where other experts cause wrong cases due to erroneous expert conclusions, they shall bear legal liability according to law.

Article 25 Fees for judicial expertise shall be charged in accordance with the standards approved by the competent state pricing authority.

Article 26 The Judicial Expertise Center of the People's Courts shall formulate detailed rules in accordance with these Provisions.

Article 27 These Provisions shall come into effect on the date of promulgation.

Article 28 The power to interpret these Provisions is vested in the Supreme People's Court.
